---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Recognises a `.map(new Foo())` — a map whose mapper is an OBJECT built at the
# call site rather than a lambda — and abstracts the run
# `new Foo; dup; <args>; invokespecial Foo.<init>; invokeinterface Stream.map`
# into a single Φ.hone.named-map pragma (#635, #1008).
#
# Every other map recogniser starts from a Φ.hone.lambda, which 111 mints out of
# an invokedynamic. An object mapper carries no invokedynamic at all, so 111
# never fires, no lambda is ever formed, and the operation was invisible to the
# whole 2xx pass — the gap was total, not partial. This rule is the front door
# for that shape: like 216 and 220 it lifts bare opcodes, so it matches at class
# scope to read the enclosing class name, which 317 puts on the resulting
# distill so 401-fuse can compose it with its neighbours (they share one
# `class`).
#
# The object is created ONCE, when the pipeline is built, and its `apply` is
# called per element — so the fused body cannot rebuild it and cannot read it
# from the caller's locals either, since 511/512 lift that body into a method of
# its own. It is, in exactly the sense 112-114 mean, a CAPTURE: 317 appends the
# constructed object to the shared state List in the `state` block and reads it
# back per element through a Φ.hone.fetch, so a named map fuses with a capturing
# one, with a distinct and with a skip on 401's blind state+body join.
#
# The element type cannot be read off anything here. A lambda carries an
# instantiated descriptor that 202 seds the element type out of; `Foo` declares
# its type argument in its OWN class file, which phino never sees, and the call
# site is erased to (Ljava/lang/Object;)Ljava/lang/Object;. So all four type
# fields are pinned to Ljava/lang/Object; — the same erasure a distinct or a skip
# carries — and 247 narrows them when the next pragma says what it accepts.
#
# The constructor may CAPTURE (#1008). `new Foo(k)` pushes its arguments between
# the `dup` and the `invokespecial`, so the run is no longer a fixed four
# opcodes; 𝐵-args binds that stretch whole and 𝑒-init takes the descriptor the
# `()V` literal used to pin. Nothing is peeled, and that is the difference from a
# capturing LAMBDA: 114 peels a lambda's pushes because its captures are passed
# to lambda$ on every element, while these are constructor arguments evaluated
# once. 317 splices 𝐵-args back between the `new` and the `<init>` inside the
# `state` block, which 502 relocates into the caller — the method the pushes came
# from, where their locals are still in scope and still hold what javac meant.
#
# Binding 𝑒-object on both the `new` and the `<init>` owner is what proves the
# two belong together now that the descriptor no longer does. It also settles a
# nested `new Foo(new Bar())`: the inner run sits inside 𝐵-args, and the split
# that would pair `new Foo` with `Bar.<init>` cannot bind one 𝑒-object to two
# names.
#
# THE ARGUMENTS ARE NOT BOUNDED, and the `when` guard that used to bound them to
# two JVM slots was reasoning from the wrong base (#1029). It read: `502` bumps
# the caller's max-stack by a fixed 6, this append peaks at four cells with no
# arguments, so two more slots reach the guarantee and a third overflows it. The
# arithmetic is right and the premise is wrong — it measures the append against
# nothing, when javac has already measured most of it.
#
# The run this rule lifts is javac's own. `new Foo; dup; <args>; invokespecial`
# stood right here, at this very stack depth, in the class jeo disassembled, so
# `max_stack` was sized for its peak: with the source stream below it the
# original reached `[.., stream, Foo, Foo, <args>]`. `502` relocates the same
# opcodes to the same point and puts them above the ArrayList it builds, so the
# fused peak is `[.., stream, list, list, Foo, Foo, <args>]` — the identical run
# with TWO more cells under it. The arguments cancel out of the difference
# whatever they are, one slot or nine, and so does whatever transient depth the
# argument expressions reach on their way: javac counted that too, at the same
# base.
#
# So a relocated append needs `max_stack + 2`, and `502` gives it 6. The 6 is
# sized for `307`'s and `308`'s appends, which build a HashSet and a `long[]`
# that were never in the original bytecode and therefore have no number of
# javac's to inherit. It covers this one several times over. `new Wide(a, b, c)`
# with an int, a long and a double — six slots of arguments — fuses and loads:
# its method declared `stack=8` for the unfused form and the fused prologue
# peaks at 10.
#
# A mapper that is LOADED rather than built — out of a parameter, a local or a
# static field — is `215-recognize-ref-map`'s (#1014). It is a different shape
# rather than a wider version of this one: there is no `new` to anchor on, so it
# keys on the stream call's descriptor and takes the opcode adjacent to it. What
# the two share is everything downstream — the same pragma, differing only in
# what its `make` block holds.
name: recognize-named-map
pattern: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-new ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.new,
          𝜏1 ↦ 𝑒-object
        ⟧,
        𝜏-dup ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.dup
        ⟧,
        𝐵-args,
        𝜏-init ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokespecial,
          𝜏2 ↦ 𝑒-object,
          𝜏3 ↦ "<init>",
          𝜏4 ↦ 𝑒-init,
          𝜏5 ↦ Φ.false
        ⟧,
        𝜏-invokeinterface ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokeinterface,
          𝜏6 ↦ "java/util/stream/Stream",
          𝜏7 ↦ "map",
          𝜏8 ↦ "(Ljava/util/function/Function;)Ljava/util/stream/Stream;",
          𝜏9 ↦ Φ.true
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-named ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.hone.named-map,
          class ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
          make ↦ ⟦
            𝜏-make-new ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.new, i1 ↦ 𝑒-object ⟧,
            𝜏-make-dup ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.dup ⟧,
            𝐵-args,
            𝜏-make-init ↦ ⟦
              φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokespecial,
              i2 ↦ 𝑒-object,
              i3 ↦ "<init>",
              i4 ↦ 𝑒-init,
              i5 ↦ Φ.false
            ⟧
          ⟧,
          bridge-input ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          bridge-output ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          accepted ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          returned ↦ "Ljava/lang/Object;",
          static ↦ "true"
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail
  ⟧
where:
  - meta: 𝜏-named
    function: random-tau
  - meta: 𝜏-make-new
    function: random-tau
  - meta: 𝜏-make-dup
    function: random-tau
  - meta: 𝜏-make-init
    function: random-tau
